{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And she continued, etc.: et his brother et Hevel.</b> The reason it was necessary to say ״et his brother״ seemingly without need, and also to elaborate by saying the word <b>et</b> twice, is explained according to the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 22): for after Hevel had prevailed over Cain and felled him, Hevel took pity on him and rose from upon him, and afterward Cain rose, etc., and killed him (Tanchuma, Bereshit 9). This is what Scripture alludes to in saying <b>et his brother</b> — meaning that he conducted himself toward Cain with the trait of brotherhood, as mentioned; but Cain conducted himself toward him with no brotherhood, but rather as toward a stranger. And this is what is meant by <b>et Hevel</b> with no brotherhood, and therefore He separated them with two instances of et. Further, the two instances of et allude, according to the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Yevamot 62b), to the fact that two twin sisters were born with Hevel; therefore it says et twice, while for Cain it says et only once, for only one was born with him."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:07.076510861Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}